From adb1174f8828e3bbd3851eed0e122bf65a9deb3b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bi Wu <biwu85@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 00:21:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] vmctx: native VM contexts -- run a program whose memory and
 kernel live on another machine

A VM context is a first-class Linux process whose instructions execute
under hardware virtualization (SVM here) while its address space is
owned and served elsewhere. This kernel provides:

- The vmctx_run/vmctx_ctl syscalls: context lifecycle, a monitor that
  services a context's events (faults, syscalls, exceptions), register
  and memory access, page take/serve/land/protect operations, and
  assisted syscalls executed in the context's own task, where the
  program's arguments mean what the program meant.

- The per-address-space page record: an xarray beside the page tables
  holding each page's ownership state (HOME/REMOTE/CLAIM/TRANSIT), with
  fault-path claims taken before the monitor is asked and sibling
  faults waiting in-kernel for a transition in flight.

- Process-lifecycle integration: fork copies the context and records
  the child's host-namespace pid for the monitor; exec marks the
  context for rebuild; exit routes teardown through the protocol; the
  native robust-futex walk and clear-child-tid run in order through
  exit_mm_release while the source's memory service is still alive.

- The mm-mutation change log: every vacated range of a context's
  address space is stamped by a per-mm event counter at the event and
  drained by the monitor, through one refcounted MMU-notifier
  subscription per address space, so destruction propagates by what the
  mm did, never by a list of syscall numbers.

- Guarded memory supply: a context's memory is never invented locally.
  Foreign reads and attributed third-party writes of missing pages are
  refused; shmem hole allocation on a live context's object is refused
  to foreign readers; a fault the monitor declined that the local
  kernel then cannot serve is reported a second time before the context
  is ended. A census at handle_mm_fault accounts every path into a
  context's memory.

The source-side contracts added by the September 2026 audit (builds
#182-#222 of this tree, deployed as 7.0.14-vmctx-audit-recall1-terminal222):

- Architectural CPU state: GETCPU/SETCPU carry the complete state in the
  adapter's own XSAVE layout; the guest runs a negotiated generic x86
  CPU model (CPUID answered from the model, guest XCR0 follows it, host
  XCR0 restored before host interrupts, a generic vendor identity, ELF
  hardware flags restricted to it, /proc/cpuinfo reporting the
  negotiated features to a context's descriptors). Exceptions and debug
  traps are delivered in the source kernel; clocks stay with the source.

- Execution CPU time (RUNTIME/BOUNDARY): the executor's measured guest
  nanoseconds are credited to the source task's public CPU clocks,
  exited-thread totals and CPU-timer caches; a source budget arms
  checkpoints that a native repeating timer forces even on tickless
  CPUs, and a taken checkpoint is killable.

- Native syscall admission: BEGIN runs entry tracing and seccomp on the
  canonical frame, QUERY publishes the admitted call, COMMIT dispatches
  once; tickets fence retries, and fork/vfork publish the committed
  child before the parent returns.

- Retained identities: monitor-owned context descriptors keep task and
  MM identity, terminal status and committed children across PID reuse
  (ABI 2 MM-lifetime queries, exact-task SIGNAL); an unreleased RESTORE
  is bound to its monitor's task lifetime; INFO/OPEN are allowed while
  an assisted syscall waits for I/O; execution backing objects are
  retained and replaced under an epoch fence; MEMORY commands carry the
  expected MM and are refused before inner argument access otherwise.

- Transfer custody: thread-owned recall tickets and page guards; capture
  reserves before it copies, keeps replay bytes until the exact
  installation acknowledgement, retains failure state until native
  cancellation or abandonment, and abandons calling-thread custody
  before exit waits; a capture is refused over an incumbent fault
  CLAIM/TRANSIT under the same MM lock fault admission uses (#221);
  read-only private pages transfer custody through a forced COW that
  preserves file and fork aliases, then TAKE; terminal death and
  monitor attachment are read under one ev_lock section so a retained
  QUERY keeps its authorization across native exit (#222).

- Executor-side operations this tree also carries: MAPPING (the VMA and
  a monitor-owned reference to its file), PROTECT_MM and
  EXECUTOR_MAP_CAPS, SET_SHARED_PAGE, MAPOBJ_READ, quiesce leases with
  PROTECT_BACKING for snapshots, mprotect preserving existing write
  restrictions (MM_CP_PRESERVE_WRITE), and the optional vmctx_map_trace
  record of MM lifetimes and mapping commits.

- HMM: owned device-private PTE/PMD write checks, and test_hmm draining
  its device reference before unload (a substrate probe for the native
  residency representation, not integrated with vmctx transport).

Known open, measured and recorded in lan-boot's vmctx/audit/: remote
residency is still an ordinary absent PTE beside a separate record, so
UFFDIO_COPY/ZEROPAGE, MADV_COLLAPSE and MREMAP_FIXED can install or
lose a remotely owned page; foreign GUP does not recall source custody;
secondary-TLB revocation is not atomic-safe.

Companion userspace (vmhome/vmremote), the controls and the test suite
live in the lan-boot repository; this tree's delta ships there as
kernel-patches/linux-7.0.14.
